Standing there at a carnival, I'd be a little suspicious right off the bat. Carnival games are designed to fool you, so a coin I've never seen coming up heads twice in a row makes me wonder if it's rigged to favor heads, or even double-headed. Two heads isn't a lot of evidence on its own, but the carnival setting nudges me toward thinking something might be off rather than assuming it's perfectly fair.

So my gut says heads is somewhat more likely than not for the next flip — not overwhelmingly, since two flips could easily happen by chance with a fair coin, but enough that I'd lean toward heads showing up again.

{"rationale": "Two heads at a carnival hint the coin might be biased toward heads, so I'd lean that way.", "p_heads": 0.7}

Okay, so I've seen this coin do its thing twice, and both times it landed heads. That's not a lot to go on, but it's not nothing either. At a carnival, part of me is suspicious — these games can be rigged, and a coin that keeps coming up heads might be weighted that way on purpose. But I also know two flips is a tiny sample, and even a perfectly fair coin would land heads twice in a row pretty often, so I can't read too much into it.

My gut says the coin is probably a bit more likely to come up heads than tails, just because the only evidence I have points that way, and the carnival setting nudges me toward thinking it might favor heads. But I wouldn't bet heavily on it — two heads just isn't enough to convince me it's strongly biased. So I'd lean a little above an even chance, expecting heads is somewhat more likely than not.

